es  we also offer a model based analysis for  time series change point and anomaly  detection  to to indicate  abrupt and significant changes  so our platform provides our users the  access to all these metrics  analysis through various interactive  interfaces like  visualization dashboards api  sql interface and alerts  with the capabilities that our platform  offers our users can continuously make  sure that  their models are performing well they  can use the interactive interfaces to  derive signals to retain a model  proactively make decisions on whether  they want to use a feature or not  or explain why their model has shifted  so to offer the services  or a service to multiple teams and  models  we need to make sure that our platform  is able to handle the scalability from  both functional and non-functional  requirements perspective  to achieve that we made these five key  design decisions  while building a platform the first  is that the features are pre-registered  in a feature catalog so that we can  identify them  and determine how to access them  we're also making sure that the features  are sorted in standardized format  so that we can read them easily  the second design decision was to have  our clients  or client users and applications  get their own access to the features  they want to monitor and provide that to  the platform so that  the platform doesn't have to have access  to massive volume of features in capital  one  this was definitely a challenging  problem to solve  but it was a top priority as a bank  the third decision is to enable our  users to specify their own  aggregation keys without this capability  to tailor our outputs  to the context that the model developers  are working with  the metrics and analysis that we produce  will likely be  completely useless  our fourth decision was to make sure  that we build a system in a way that our  users  aren't stepping on each other's toes by  incorporating  distributed processing techniques and  robust orchestration layer  and lastly because qualifying a feature  drift  is very specific to each model we  provide tools to visualize  slice and dice the metrics and analysis  that our produce  platform produces to empower users to  make their  own conclusion decisions on when and  what to do  with their models so next here and i  are going to double click on the  implementation details of these design  decisions  so the first thing we'll cover is the  data pipeline architecture  this is essentially what makes various  features discoverable  and readable to the feature monitoring  platform  so to my team's luck capital one has a  reliable and robust enterprise common  capability for data cataloging  data ingestion and persistent storage  that almost all applications use already  so with these services the feature  compute functions can calculate features  in  just in time streaming or batch fashion  and send the computer output to the  ingestion service  for the data to be persisted in rk files  with  file location partition keys  and schema available in the data catalog  service  the ingestion service can take average  messages from streaming  and just-in-time api services or whole  files from bad systems  thanks sorry so now i am going to talk  uh about the technical details of our  feature monitoring as a service  uh this is our architecture diagram so  we have a trigger api as an enter point  to our  monitoring service also the trigger api  connects to the air flow  and the spins up emr cluster that runs a  pi spark job  you know you may notice that we also  have a radius cluster  um the need for that is to cache our api  requests  so that we don't accidentally receive  like five  ten same requests and spins up too many  emr cluster  um the stats computation are done by  spark  emr and the output is sent to our  enterprise kafka cluster  our enterprise kafka has the capability  of save the results  into our enterprise aws s3  and also we have our own ingestion  engine that listens to  our stats topic and writes the output to  postgres  as a serving layer we provide a grafana  dashboard  as well as the graphql api the other  thing i want to point out is  we um we have external team that builds  machine learning as a service  we partner with them so they can use the  stats we produced  and run more advanced analysis and  algorithm on top of that  to do a feature drift detection  excited so  um yeah the first thing i'm going to  talk about is  our trigger api so it triggers and  configures our feature statistics  calculation  as nori mentioned that we have  enterprise feature registry  it provides every feature unique id  so we receive a data set location id  from our client  and use that to query our feature  registry api  and receive a list of feature id and  that information is used for us to  uniquely identify a given feature  along with their statistics we  calculated  so besides the data set location the  data set id  and feature ids there are a couple other  configuration we  asked the user to provide us first is  the temporary client credentials and  this is what as norway mentioned that we  don't want to have a universal  credential to have access to all our  data sets  so we take this temporary credential  from our client  to read their feature data set for our  stats computation job  um and it's like more secure that way so  that  um we don't um so we have like better  design um pattern so as we are  designing the whole process we realize  there are various  aspects of time for a given data set for  for example i have this um data set on  on the right hand side  let's say if we want to track someone's  biking exercise we want to track like  how far he he has gone and like what is  that elevation gain for  a biking trip so there's the concept of  event time meaning that when was that  biking trip occurred  and then there's also the concept of eto  time meaning  that when does that data set landed in  our system  so as a data engineer maybe i'm more  curious of future statistics based on  the etl time  and just to see if my etl process is  running correctly  of if there's any major change of the  process  but however as a data scientist maybe  i'm more interested in the  statistics that's aggregated based on  event time for example if i want to run  some analysis  for a given user and see like oh what's  the average  biking length for that user for the past  seven  or three months so because of that we  provide this additional field called  aggregation fields it lets us  lets the user configure on what field  they want  to use to produce these statistics  in that way our pipeline is a lot more  flexible of the statistic  statistics we're generating  so now i'm going to talk a little bit  more about  our stats calculation so our stats  calculation process is  running on emr and as norma mentioned  that right now we compute some  descriptive  statistics like mean max average and  some quantiles and counts  we also compute psi the population  stability index  just to kind of measure the distribution  between the two data sets  the default behavior of our pipeline  right now is to compare  the latest partition of a data set  versus the partition before  so all our processes are running on emr  and every individual request it has its  own emr  um in that way so there's like no  processes interfering with  each other and  also all the results are sent to our  enterprise kafka  and saved to our postgres database as  well as s3  one thing i want to mention that we also  have a unique job id  i didn't associate with every individual  um  stats calculation it's easier to  in that way it's easier for us to keep  track of um  the calculation process like how far the  process has run  and also it's easier for us to debug and  see like if  stat job has completed or not  so um next i'm going to talk a little  bit about our postgres table design  so we have two tables to keep track of  